  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Winds will be gusty at times through Monday morning. \n\nWinds will continue to be gusty at times through Monday \nmorning, with boundary layer flow gradually veering to the WNW \novernight. Gusts of 20 to 35 mph will be possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cool and unsettled with periods of lake enhanced \nrain showers through Monday morning.\n\nA progressive and potent mid level trough will dig from the upper \nGreat Lakes tonight to New England by Monday, with an associated \ncold front crossing the eastern Great Lakes tonight. The airmass is \ncool enough to support a lake response ahead of the cold front. A \nfew lake effect rain showers should develop east/northeast of the \nlakes by early this evening as forcing and synoptic scale moisture \nincrease. Initial development will be near Buffalo and Watertown on \nWSW flow.\n\nBoundary layer flow will veer to the WNW tonight and NW by Monday \nmorning, carrying lake effect and lake enhanced rain showers \nsoutheast of the lakes. Synoptic scale forcing will be strong \nenough to support scattered to numerous showers even outside of \nlake effect areas overnight through early Monday. Lake enhancement\nwill bring steadier rain and higher QPF east and southeast of the\nlakes, with the highest rain amounts across the Tug Hill and \nOswego County. A few locations may pick up 0.50\" to 0.75\" of rain,\nwith the steady translation of lake effect bands southward in \nveering wind profiles preventing any higher totals. Boundary \nlayer temperatures stay warm enough for all liquid, although a \nfew locations may see some graupel or small hail in convective \nlake effect showers.\n\nShowers will continue through Monday morning, especially southeast \nof the lakes from ongoing lake enhancement and upslope flow. The mid \nlevel trough and associated deeper moisture will then pull away in \nthe afternoon, with showers gradually ending from west to east. The \nlast of the showers across Central NY and the higher terrain of the \nNorth Country will mostly end by mid to late afternoon.",
